To find the matrix for projecting onto the vector $\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}$, we first normalize the vector. This can be done by dividing each element by the magnitude of the vector.

\begin{align*}
\text{magnitude} &= \sqrt{(-3)^2 + (-2)^2}\\
&= \sqrt{9 + 4}\\
&= \sqrt{13}\\
\end{align*}

Therefore, the normalized vector is $\begin{pmatrix} -3/sqrt(13) \\ -2/sqrt(13) \end{pmatrix}$. The projection matrix onto this vector is

\begin{align*}
\begin{pmatrix}
-3/\sqrt{13} & -2/\sqrt{13} \\
-2/\sqrt{13} & -3/\sqrt{13}
\end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}

Then it is easier to find its inverse using the following equation:

Let $A$ be the projection matrix. $A^{-1} = A^{T}(\sqrt{det(A)})$

Therefore, $A^{T}=\begin{pmatrix}
-\frac{3}{\sqrt{13}} & -\frac{2}{\sqrt{13}} \\
-\frac{2}{\sqrt{13}} & -\frac{3}{\sqrt{13}}
\end{pmatrix}$ Since $\sqrt{det(A)}=(\pm 1)$

$A^{T}(\pm 1)=\begin{pmatrix}
-\frac{3}{\sqrt{13}} & -\frac{2}{\sqrt{13}} \\
-\frac{2}{\sqrt{13}} & -\frac{3}{\sqrt{13}}
\end{pmatrix}$ Therefore,

\begin{align*}
A^{-1} &= \frac{1}{(\pm 1)}
\end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}

Therefore, the inverse of the projection matrix does not exist.

